WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
Six warehouses, same exact product,

2
00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.360
and the exact same safety stock

3
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.560
sitting in every single one of them.

4
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.560
Listen, you basically just paid

5
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.640
for the exact same protection six times over.

6
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.040
Holding localized safety stock everywhere like that,

7
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.920
well, you're essentially just funding

8
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.840
your competitor's growth at that point.

9
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.320
So how do we fix it?

10
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.560
It all comes down to this brilliant concept

11
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.160
called risk pooling.

12
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.760
The idea is when you centralize your inventory,

13
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.840
the demand variability of all those independent locations

14
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.680
kind of just partially cancels each other out.

15
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.040
The math here is absolutely crucial.

16
00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.960
The total buffer you need for the whole network

17
00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.320
is actually way less than the sum of those isolated locations.

18
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.160
Why?

19
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.120
Because it scales with the square root,

20
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.360
not the literal count.

21
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.120
It's a real game changer.

22
00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.640
Now, I'm not saying central

23
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.240
is always automatically better.

24
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.280
It's a strategic trade-off.

25
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.280
Think of it like a control panel with two dials.

26
00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.680
On one hand, centralizing dramatically

27
00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.520
slashes your carrying costs,

28
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.000
but yeah, it does add outbound lead time to the store.

29
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.120
So what's the practical GCC answer to this exact trade-off?

30
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.840
You hold that pooled buffer in a central Jopsa hub, right?

31
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.520
Then you use it to feed regional retail nodes

32
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.200
that hold only cycle stock

33
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.880
plus a super thin local cover.

34
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.560
That way, you get the pooling benefit

35
00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.680
without ever starving the shelf.

36
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.880
And the payoff?

37
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:25.440
If you model this correctly,

38
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.120
this framework yields roughly a 25% lower carrying cost,

39
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.240
all while maintaining a gold standard 98% service level,

40
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.160
literally the exact same service,

41
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.800
but with way less cash tied up.

42
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.920
So for those of you actually signing the inventory budgets,

43
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.440
please model this out

44
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.160
before you even think about signing that next warehouse lease.

45
00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.600
Stop bleeding cash on redundant inventory.

46
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.080
I'm Badiak Bhadani, demand planning and AI Dubai.
